  en: {
    "title": "Shrimp Life Cycle from Larvae to Harvest",
    "desc": `The life cycle of shrimp, particularly vannamei shrimp (Litopenaeus vannamei), consists of several stages starting from spawning to harvest readiness. Each stage has its own unique and fascinating characteristics. The stages are as follows:
  <br><br>
  1. <b>Egg</b><br><br>
  a. <b>Description:</b><br>
        A female shrimp can produce between 500,000 to 1,000,000 eggs in a single spawning.
  <br><br>
  b. <b>Fun Fact:</b><br>
        These eggs hatch within 12–16 hours after being released, producing very small and microscopic larvae.
  <br><br>
  
  2. <b>Nauplius</b><br><br>
  a. <b>Description:</b><br>
        After hatching, the larvae enter the nauplius stage, during which they do not require external food because they rely on the yolk sac for nourishment.
  <br><br>
  b. <b>Fun Fact:</b><br>
        Larvae go through six molting stages during this phase, which lasts about 30–50 hours.
  <br><br>
  
  3. <b>Zoea</b><br><br>
  a. <b>Description:</b><br>
        This phase occurs after the nauplius stage and lasts for 4–5 days, during which the larvae begin feeding on phytoplankton and zooplankton.
  <br><br>
  b. <b>Fun Fact:</b><br>
        In the zoea stage, the larvae molt three times and can reach a size of 1.05 to 3.30 mm.
  <br><br>
  
  4. <b>Mysis</b><br><br>
  a. <b>Description:</b><br>
        After the zoea stage, the larvae enter the mysis stage, where they begin to resemble small shrimp.
  <br><br>
  b. <b>Fun Fact:</b><br>
        At this stage, the larvae become more active and start searching for food independently.
  <br><br>
  
  5. <b>Post Larvae</b><br><br>
  a. <b>Description:</b><br>
        In the post-larval (PL) stage, shrimp begin to take on the form of adult shrimp. The post-larval stage is usually labeled PL1, PL2, and so on based on age.
  <br><br>
  b. <b>Fun Fact:</b><br>
        At this stage, shrimp can be transferred to grow-out ponds for cultivation after reaching 20 to 25 days old.
  <br><br>
  
  6. <b>Juvenile</b><br><br>
  a. <b>Description:</b><br>
        After passing the post-larval stage, shrimp become juveniles with an appearance almost identical to that of adult shrimp.
  <br><br>
  b. <b>Fun Fact:</b><br>
        The juvenile phase is critical, as shrimp require a suitable habitat to ensure optimal growth.
  <br><br>
  
  7. <b>Young Shrimp</b><br><br>
  a. <b>Description:</b><br>
        Within approximately 80–90 days after the juvenile stage, shrimp develop into young shrimp ready for further cultivation.
  <br><br>
  b. <b>Fun Fact:</b><br>
        The weight of young shrimp greatly depends on the cultivation methods used in the pond.
  <br><br>
  
  8. <b>Adult Shrimp</b><br><br>
  a. <b>Description:</b><br>
        About 25–30 days after becoming young shrimp, they reach adulthood and are ready for harvest.
  <br><br>
  b. <b>Fun Fact:</b><br>
        Shrimp are considered mature after around four months from the juvenile stage and have high economic value at this point.
  <br><br>
  
  By applying the tips and tricks above, you can manage your aquaculture activities more effectively and increase your chances of success and profit from this venture. Good luck!
  <br><br>
  By understanding the life cycle of vannamei shrimp, farmers can optimize care and growing environments to achieve maximum harvest yields.
  <br>
        <b>Closing:</b> Understanding the life cycle of vannamei shrimp from larvae to adulthood is the first step toward achieving optimal harvest results. However, successful farming also greatly depends on pond environment quality. The following two modern technologies can help improve productivity:<br><br>
  <b>Eco Aerator</b><br>
  Helps maintain optimal dissolved oxygen levels in the pond, which is crucial for shrimp growth at every stage of life. The Eco Aerator is energy-efficient, easy to use, and capable of creating a healthier aquatic ecosystem.
  <br><br>
  <b>Ebii System</b><br>
        This automated system provides a complete solution for water quality monitoring. With the Ebii System, you can measure key parameters such as pH, temperature, and salinity in real-time. This makes it easier to maintain an ideal environment for shrimp.<br><br>
  Investing in the right technology not only makes your work easier but also ensures better and more abundant harvests. Start using the Eco Aerator and Ebii System now to take your shrimp farming to the next level!<br><br>`
  ,
    "tags": ["ShrimpFarming", "Technology", "Crustea"]
} 
